**🟧 HIGH** · Bug — **Cancelled-reminder cron deletes DB rows but leaves the SendGrid batch ID uncancelled for unscheduled reminders, and one failure aborts the whole batch**

The cron job awaits `client.request` inside a loop, and the Prisma deletes are only built as promises and awaited at the end. If one SendGrid call throws, the catch block exits the whole loop. The remaining cancelled reminders are not processed and the error is only logged. Reminders whose cancel call already succeeded are never deleted, because their delete promises are never awaited. Prisma queries are lazy, so un-awaited promises never execute. On the next run those reminders are cancelled again, which is wasteful and can fail.

The query also has no `referenceId: { not: null }` filter. A reminder with a null `referenceId` sends `batch_id: null` to SendGrid. It would be better to wrap each reminder in its own try/catch and delete the row right after its successful cancel.

**Proposed fix:**

~~~ts
for (const reminder of remindersToCancel) {
  if (!reminder.referenceId) continue;
  try {
    await client.request({ url: "/v3/user/scheduled_sends", method: "POST", body: { batch_id: reminder.referenceId, status: "cancel" } });
    await prisma.workflowReminder.delete({ where: { id: reminder.id } });
  } catch (error) {
    console.log(`Error cancelling scheduled Email: ${error}`);
  }
}
~~~

**🟧 HIGH** · Bug — **`getCalendarCredentials` stores un-awaited calendar promises; sync consumers break**

`getCalendar` now returns a Promise, but `getCalendarCredentials` still calls it synchronously, so each `calendar` field is a Promise. `getConnectedCalendars` awaits it, but any other consumer of `getCalendarCredentials` (outside this diff) that treats `calendar` as a `Calendar` will get a Promise and fail at runtime. TypeScript may not catch this if the types are inferred loosely. Callers must be checked and the type made explicit, or the calendar resolved inside this function.

**Proposed fix:**

~~~ts
// make the field type explicit and audit all callers of getCalendarCredentials
// or resolve before returning:
const calendar = await getCalendar(credential);
~~~

**🟧 HIGH** · Bug — **Working-hours check compares start to itself and inverts the logic**

`end` is computed from `slotStartTime` (a copy-paste error), so it never accounts for the event length. The `find` callback returns true when the slot is outside a single working-hour entry, and that makes the slot unavailable. A user with several working-hour entries on one day, for example 9-12 and 14-17, would have every slot rejected because each slot falls outside at least one of the entries. Working hours are also stored in the organizer's timezone, but the slot is compared in UTC with no conversion. This is wrong for any non-UTC organizer and can wrongly drop valid slots. The fix is to check that the slot lies inside at least one working-hour entry (`some`), using slot end = start + eventLength and converting to the organizer timezone.

**Proposed fix:**

~~~ts
const local = time.tz(organizerTimeZone);
const start = local.hour()*60 + local.minute();
const end = start + eventLength;
const inWorking = workingHours.some(w => w.days.includes(local.day()) && start >= w.startTime && end <= w.endTime);
if (workingHours.length && !inWorking) return false;
~~~

**🟨 MEDIUM** · Bug — **Override offset logic in getSlots is wrong when the offset crosses a day boundary**

The override start and end times are shifted by the offset between the invitee and organizer timezones, then reduced to minutes of the UTC day. A shift across midnight wraps the hour and can make `endTime < startTime`, or can place the override on the wrong day. `override.start.toString()` is also passed through `dayjs` for no reason and can be parsed in the local timezone. `override.timeZone` may be undefined, and `.tz(undefined)` then silently uses the local zone. The shifted value is computed twice per field.

**Proposed fix:**

~~~ts
const s = dayjs(override.start).utc().add(offset, "minute");
const e = dayjs(override.end).utc().add(offset, "minute");
// guard timeZone fallback and handle day wrap
~~~

**🟨 MEDIUM** · Bug — **Shared mutable `rejectedEmails` array across controller instances**

`Em.A([])` in `extend` is a prototype-level property shared by all instances. State persists across instances and is never reset. Initialize it in `init` instead.

**Proposed fix:**

~~~js
init: function() {
  this._super();
  this.set('rejectedEmails', Em.A([]));
},
~~~

**🟨 MEDIUM** · Bug — **Header `.title` float removed and `.contents` made flex, which may break the layout**

The `.title` rule with `float: left` was removed, and `.contents` is now a flex container. The `.panel` float was also replaced with `margin-left: auto` and `order: 3`. Floats have no effect on flex items, so this part is harmless. The risk is that other rules for `.title`, such as the logo or `.valign-helper`, may depend on the old block/float behaviour, and I cannot see them in the diff. The `order` values are also inconsistent. `.panel` gets 3 and `.extra-info-wrapper` gets 2, but the title has no explicit order and so defaults to 0. This works only by accident, and the ordering is fragile if `.extra-info-wrapper` is not inside `.contents`. `.extra-info-wrapper` also keeps `float: left` for its `.badge-wrapper` children, which is unrelated to this change. Verify the header visually and give all siblings explicit orders.

**Proposed fix:**

~~~scss
.title { @include order(1); }
.extra-info-wrapper { @include order(2); }
.panel { @include order(3); margin-left: auto; }
~~~

**🟦 LOW** · Bug — **Invalid or incorrect legacy prefix values in the `align-items` mixin**

`-webkit-box-align` takes the values `start`, `end`, `center`, `baseline` and `stretch`, so it does not accept `flex-start` or `flex-end`. `-ms-flex-align` uses `start` and `end` in the same way. Passing `flex-start` produces invalid declarations that browsers ignore. `-ms-align-items` is not a real property. `-moz-box-align` is missing. The indentation is inconsistent, and line 120 has trailing whitespace. The current calls (`center` and `baseline`) work. The mixin is unsafe for other values, and the bogus property is dead code.

**Proposed fix:**

~~~scss
@mixin align-items($alignment) {
  -webkit-box-align: $alignment;
  -moz-box-align: $alignment;
  -ms-flex-align: $alignment;
  -webkit-align-items: $alignment;
  align-items: $alignment;
}
~~~

**🟧 HIGH** · Bug — **Existing devices are rejected when the limit is reached because the update filter is too narrow**

The update path uses `WHERE device_id = ? AND updated_at BETWEEN (UpdatedAt-30d) AND (UpdatedAt+1m)`. A device that exists but has an `updated_at` outside that window is treated as absent. That includes rows with a newer `updated_at`, such as a concurrent update, or rows older than 30 days that have not been purged yet. `rowsAffected == 0` is then reported as `ErrDeviceLimitReached`, so the user is denied.
MySQL also reports 0 affected rows when the new values equal the existing ones, unless `clientFoundRows` is set. An existing device that re-sends identical data would then be wrongly rejected as over the limit.
Fix: distinguish "not found" from "no change". Check existence with a SELECT, or drop the lower-bound filter and match on `device_id` only.

**Proposed fix:**

~~~go
// UPDATE ... WHERE device_id = ?
// if rowsAffected == 0, SELECT COUNT(*) WHERE device_id = ?;
// return ErrDeviceLimitReached only if the device does not exist
~~~

**🟨 MEDIUM** · Bug — **SQLite parameter-limit workaround is keyed on the configured batch size instead of the ID count, and is fragile**

The limit is 999 only for SQLite older than 3.32; newer versions allow 32766. The code switches to inlined IDs whenever the batch size exceeds 999, so the workaround covers most configurations. The check should be on `len(ids)`, which is what matters. The comment is also inaccurate. Inlining is safe here because the IDs are int64. Still, `fmt.Sprintf` in a loop is O(n²) string concatenation. Use `strings.Builder` or `strings.Join`.

**Proposed fix:**

~~~go
if r.db.GetDBType() == migrator.SQLite && len(ids) > sqliteParameterLimit {
    parts := make([]string, len(ids))
    for i, v := range ids { parts[i] = strconv.FormatInt(v, 10) }
    sql = fmt.Sprintf(`DELETE FROM %s WHERE id IN (%s)`, table, strings.Join(parts, ","))
}
~~~

**🟧 HIGH** · Bug — **Client "all-clients" check can NPE and grants permission on the wrong resource**

When no per-client resource exists, the code loads the type resource via `getResourceTypeResource` and calls `findByResource(server, resource)` without a null check. If the resource is null, this throws an NPE. If it is non-null but has no policies, the method returns false. When policies do exist, evaluation proceeds on the type-level resource. That is correct only if the type resource's scopes are what the evaluation expects. The evaluation uses `resource.getScopes()`, which depends on how that resource was created. A client that has no resource of its own, but is covered by an all-clients permission, relies on this fallback. It is also done differently from `hasPermission(String)`, which looks up the type resource by name. The two code paths are inconsistent, so one of them is likely wrong.

**Proposed fix:**

~~~java
if (resource == null) {
    resource = AdminPermissionsSchema.SCHEMA.getResourceTypeResource(session, server, CLIENTS_RESOURCE_TYPE);
    if (resource == null || policyStore.findByResource(server, resource).isEmpty()) return false;
}
~~~

**🟨 MEDIUM** · Bug — **Unsupported V2 methods throw UnsupportedOperationException on reachable code paths**

`isPermissionsEnabled`, `setPermissionsEnabled`, `resource`, `getPermissions` and similar methods throw in V2. The `ClientRemovedEvent` listener calls `clients().setPermissionsEnabled(client, false)`. The V2 `clients()` is now used when the feature is enabled, so deleting any client would throw `UnsupportedOperationException`. This breaks client deletion, including in `testManageAllClients`. The listener is also gated only on the `ADMIN_FINE_GRAINED_AUTHZ` feature. The same risk applies to the REST endpoints for client permissions, such as `ClientResource.getManagementPermissions`. Make the removal handler V2-aware, and make `setPermissionsEnabled` a no-op or return a sensible value.

**Proposed fix:**

~~~java
// in V2: make setPermissionsEnabled a no-op, or skip it in the listener when V2 is active
@Override public void setPermissionsEnabled(ClientModel c, boolean e) { }
~~~

**🟧 HIGH** · Bug — **Changing the recreate exit code from 4 to 3 may break existing consumers**

`RECREATE_UPGRADE_EXIT_CODE` changed from 4 to 3, and 4 now means "feature disabled". Anything that hard-codes the old value (the operator's upgrade logic, scripts, tests, other docs) will misread results. In particular, a disabled feature (4) would be read as "recreate" by any consumer still checking for 4, which could lead to wrong update decisions. The visible docs table lists 3 as "recreate", so it is consistent there. The diff does not show whether the operator code or the other tests that interpret the exit code were updated. Search for literal uses of 3 and 4 and confirm that all consumers use the constants.

**Proposed fix:**

~~~java
// Verify all callers (operator, tests, docs) use CompatibilityResult constants;
// grep for literal exit code 4 / 3 handling and update accordingly.
~~~

**🟨 MEDIUM** · Implementation — **Feature check runs without the feature being enabled at build/runtime for the commands**

The check is duplicated in both commands, and it is placed before validateConfig(). It is also unclear whether `Profile` is initialized with the `--features` option at this point. Since the docs now pass `--features=rolling-updates` to `update-compatibility`, that option must be honored by these commands. If the profile is only built from persisted build-time options, the runtime flag may be ignored. Also, `picocli.exit(...)` followed by `return` relies on the exit code being applied later. Move the check into the shared base class to avoid drift, and verify that the feature flag is actually parsed for these commands.

**Proposed fix:**

~~~java
// In AbstractUpdatesCommand:
protected boolean featureEnabled() {
    if (!Profile.isFeatureEnabled(Profile.Feature.ROLLING_UPDATES)) {
        printFeatureDisabled();
        picocli.exit(CompatibilityResult.FEATURE_DISABLED);
        return false;
    }
    return true;
}
~~~

**🟧 HIGH** · Bug — **Recovery-code credential in user storage is stored as plaintext, and the local fallback uses different data**

The raw generated codes are serialized to JSON and passed to the user storage via updateCredential. A federated provider therefore receives the plaintext recovery codes, whereas the local path stores hashed data (credentialModel). That is a deliberate design choice for the federated case, but it is undocumented. Also, updateCredential is called on every user, even when the storage does not support the credential type. For local users this may throw or return false depending on the provider. For a local user, the updater may be a no-op; this should be verified. The code uses a hardcoded provider id string rather than RecoveryAuthnCodesCredentialProviderFactory.PROVIDER_ID. The provider is also fetched before it is known to be needed and is not null-checked.

**Proposed fix:**

~~~java
// document the plaintext hand-off to user storage; use the PROVIDER_ID constant;
// look up the provider only in the local-storage branch and null-check it
~~~

**🟨 MEDIUM** · Test — **Concurrency test is racy and may not detect or reliably reproduce the failure**

The reader thread is started but never joined. The main thread sets `deletedAll` and asserts immediately, so an exception thrown by an in-flight request after the assertion is missed. The thread can also outlive the test and keep calling the admin client after realm cleanup, which can cause flaky failures in later tests. `deletedAll` is also never set if a delete throws, so the loop would run forever. Finally, the reader may not have run a single iteration before deletion starts, so the test may pass without exercising the race. Fix: keep a reference to the thread, set the flag in a `finally` block, join the thread, then assert. Optionally use a latch so the reader has done at least one read before deletion starts.

**Proposed fix:**

~~~java
Thread reader = new Thread(...);
reader.start();
try {
    groupUuuids.forEach(id -> managedRealm.admin().groups().group(id).remove());
} finally {
    deletedAll.set(true);
    reader.join(10000);
}
assertThat(caughtExceptions, Matchers.empty());
~~~

**🟨 MEDIUM** · Bug — **Shared stopped flag and drift across processes, plus join breaks out without terminating the remaining processes**

When the deadline expires, the loop `break`s and skips `terminate()` for all remaining processes, so they leak. Also, `self.stopped.value = True` is shared by all processes, which is fine. But a process with a short deadline that is not alive still goes through the loop correctly, and the break skips the cleanup for the rest.

**Proposed fix:**

~~~python
for process in self.processes.values():
    while process.is_alive() and (deadline is None or deadline > time.time()):
        time.sleep(0.1)
    if not isinstance(process, threading.Thread):
        process.terminate()
~~~
